WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.500
Since the nuclear deal was signed a year
ago, Iran, for the most part, has kept up

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.960
the very technical terms of the deal.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.239
It has reconfigured its centrifuges; it has
filled the heavy-water reactor in Iraq

00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:26.470
that could produced plutonium; and they've
shipped out most of their

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.210
enriched uranium stockpile.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.020
So they have followed the letter of the
law when it comes to the nuclear deal

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.460
for the most part. At the same time,
they're trying to push the envelope on

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.610
what we would consider the spirit of the
deal, which is launching ballistic

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.870
missiles that could potentially deliver
nuclear weapons in the future, which Iran

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.860
says, "Hey, that's not really technically
part of the deal, so we don't have any

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.110
compunction about actually launching
missiles,"

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:54.070
no matter how much it may upset the US.
So I think what you're going to see in

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:58.000
the coming months and years as we head
into the next administration, is Iran

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.079
continuing to push the envelope on its
research and development, ballistic

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:06.429
missile testing, as well as other parts
of the nuclear program that should cause us

00:01:06.429 --> 00:01:07.929
a lot of concern.

00:01:07.929 --> 00:01:10.929
So I think this is going to be a huge
problem for the next president.

